{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787083024,"nonce":"WWBKZkqhvHVWRsaBqh3u0NbZQt0=","title":"`CasePolicyRepository.RetentionWindows` has no caller anywhere, and the per-alert lookup its comment says it exists to avoid is what the ingest path still does","message":"**What is wrong.** `RetentionWindows` — the batch form of the case-retention-window lookup, 60 lines with its own SQL, its own dedupe pass and its own map key type — is called by nothing. Not by the service, not by a port, not by a test. Its doc comment justifies its existence by naming a cost it does not remove: *\"asking per alert would put one query per alert on the hot path\"*, which is exactly what the singular form does today, once per resolved observation in the batch loop.\n\n**The demonstration.** A grep for `RetentionWindows` across the whole repository returns two lines, both inside the file that defines it: the doc comment at `internal/alerts/repository/casepolicy.go:122` and the declaration at `:129`. `CasePolicyKey`, the map key type it exists to answer on (`:185-191`), and `retentionWindowsSQL`, its unnested batch query (`:196-201`), have no other reader either.\n\nThe port does not carry it. `internal/alerts/service/ports.go:126-135` declares one method on the retention read — `RetentionWindow(ctx, s, namespace, alertname)` — and the container wires the concrete repository into `Deps.CasePolicies` at `internal/app/container.go:545,567`, so the interface is what the service can reach and the plural form is not on it.\n\nThe singular form IS on the hot path, per observation. `internal/alerts/service/lifecycle.go:387-399` sits inside the loop over a batch's observations and calls `s.caseRetention(ctx, scope, alert)` on every `TriggerObserveResolved`, under a comment that argues the narrowing is the optimisation: *\"W IS READ FOR THE ONE EDGE THAT READS IT... asking on any other trigger would put a lookup on the hottest path in oto for a value nothing would use.\"* That is a real reduction — from one query per alert to one per resolved alert — and it is a different reduction from the one `RetentionWindows` was written to make. A webhook resolving forty alerts still runs forty round trips.\n\nSo this is not a helper waiting for its first caller. It is the losing half of a decision that was made the other way, left in the tree with a comment asserting a rationale the tree contradicts.\n\n**Why it matters.** The comment is the damage, more than the code. `casepolicy.go` is one of the most carefully annotated files in the module — it explains the axes, the empty-namespace normalisation, the read-only stance, and why a missing row is not an error — and the next person reading it has no way to tell that one of its three exported members is inert. They will reasonably assume the batch path is the one ingest uses, and reason about ingest's query cost from a function that never runs. A dead function that also documents a false claim about the live path is worse than a dead function.\n\nThere is a second, quieter cost: the two forms disagree on shape. The singular returns an error for an empty alertname (`:96-102`, *\"an empty one is a programming error rather than a lookup miss\"*); the plural silently skips it (`:147-149`). If the batch form is ever wired up, that difference is a behaviour change nobody chose, hidden inside a \"no functional change\" refactor.\n\n**Where.** `internal/alerts/repository/casepolicy.go:122-183` (`RetentionWindows`), `:185-191` (`CasePolicyKey`), `:193-201` (`retentionWindowsSQL`) · `internal/alerts/service/ports.go:126-135` (the port, which declares only the singular) · `internal/app/container.go:543-547,567` (the wiring) · `internal/alerts/service/lifecycle.go:387-399` (the per-observation call to the singular form, and the comment that scopes it to T5).\n\n**Scope.** small.\n\n**Done when.** Either the batch form is deleted along with `CasePolicyKey` and `retentionWindowsSQL`, or it is wired: the port gains it, `applyChunks` resolves one map of windows per chunk before the observation loop, and `caseRetention` reads that map instead of querying. If it is deleted, nothing is left claiming that a per-alert lookup was avoided. If it is wired, the empty-alertname divergence between the two forms is reconciled first, and the singular stays for the callers that hold one pair. Either way the file no longer documents a hot-path property that the hot path does not have.","files":null}]}